00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:37.153
If you know about the 
species Lacrymaria olor,

00:00:37.487 --> 00:00:40.723
then you know what you're getting
when you see it under a microscope.

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:44.360
There's the oval body, the long neck,

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:47.230
the grasping and reaching out for food.

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:51.367
It has a distinct shape,
a distinct way of life.

00:00:51.768 --> 00:00:55.788
The combination of its own genetics
and its surrounding environment.

00:00:56.506 --> 00:00:59.058
It's also distinct that even

00:00:59.058 --> 00:01:02.896
when you look at different 
Lacrymaria olor and see that, sure,

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:08.501
these individuals don't necessarily
look exactly alike, you can still spot

00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:12.705
the underlying framework of the species
that makes them resemble each other.

00:01:13.423 --> 00:01:19.746
You see the morphology and the behavior
all united under the name Lacrymaria olor.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:27.403
It's like looking at a house cat.

00:01:28.004 --> 00:01:31.758
You know they have their own colors
and patterns and preferences,

00:01:32.141 --> 00:01:35.061
but at the same time, it makes sense

00:01:35.061 --> 00:01:38.114
why we might consider them all
to be the same species.

00:01:38.114 --> 00:01:39.999
Felis catus.

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:43.119
Then you look at the species Felix manul,

00:01:43.519 --> 00:01:45.705
better known as Pallas's cat,

00:01:46.222 --> 00:01:50.993
and the idea that these two are relatives
of each other also feels right.

00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:54.847
There is a sense of same but different.

00:01:55.515 --> 00:01:59.235
And that can make the idea of a species
seem simple enough.

00:01:59.685 --> 00:02:03.139
There are things that are like each other,
and there are things

00:02:03.139 --> 00:02:05.141
that are less like each other.

00:02:05.141 --> 00:02:08.745
So the idea of a species
is to define individuals

00:02:08.745 --> 00:02:13.282
so that we can define groups
so that we can define relationships.

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.552
Except it is never so simple

00:02:16.552 --> 00:02:19.138
when it comes
to trying to categorize life.

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:21.557
Because at the end of the day,

00:02:21.557 --> 00:02:25.511
species are only a little bit about the 
organisms we're looking at.

00:02:25.995 --> 00:02:29.265
They're really more about
us and how we see the world.

00:02:30.283 --> 00:02:33.052
Life is complicated and evolving,

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:37.607
which means it will always be pushing
against the bounds of our categorization.

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:40.960
But that doesn't mean we can't try.

00:02:41.811 --> 00:02:46.132
The human attempt to define
a species is an exercise in futility

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:49.936
that is nonetheless vital
to our understanding of biology.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:54.106
It provides us with the language
to describe the world around us

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.643
so that we can make comparisons
and find patterns.

00:02:59.145 --> 00:03:02.748
So what even is a species?

00:03:03.399 --> 00:03:05.985
Well, it depends on who you ask.

00:03:06.953 --> 00:03:11.090
There have been several species
concepts over the past few centuries.

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:16.128
One of the definitions you may be familiar
with is attributed to the zoologist

00:03:16.128 --> 00:03:20.783
Ernst Mayr who in 1942 defines species as:

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:26.055
“groups of actually or potentially
interbreeding natural populations

00:03:26.055 --> 00:03:29.675
which are reproductively
isolated from other such groups.”

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:34.997
This describes what is known as 
the Biological Species Concept.

00:03:35.564 --> 00:03:37.817
Two species might look the same,

00:03:38.167 --> 00:03:42.171
but if they can't reproduce together,
then they're different.

00:03:42.705 --> 00:03:45.524
One of the major challenges
with this definition

00:03:45.524 --> 00:03:48.594
is obvious from our journey
through the microcosmos.

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:51.464
Not all organisms interbreed.

00:03:52.064 --> 00:03:54.500
Some reproduce asexually.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:58.521
So how does one draw biological borders
around species

00:03:58.521 --> 00:04:00.890
that just make copies of themselves?

00:04:02.441 --> 00:04:05.511
There are several other species
concepts out there,

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:09.098
and we won't be able to explore
all of them in this episode.

00:04:09.498 --> 00:04:13.702
But one of the other salient definitions
that helps avoid the challenges

00:04:13.702 --> 00:04:18.891
of the Biological Species Concept
is similar to how we started this episode:

00:04:19.391 --> 00:04:22.011
Just look at the organisms.

00:04:22.011 --> 00:04:25.131
Look at their morphology,
describe their shape

00:04:25.131 --> 00:04:29.418
and color in organelles
and whatever strange features appear

00:04:29.835 --> 00:04:33.222
and use that to define
an individual species.

00:04:33.789 --> 00:04:37.660
It seems as simple as saying
that if it's almond shaped in body

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:40.663
and long and neck
then it's Lacrymaria olor.

00:04:41.113 --> 00:04:43.933
Why make it
any more complicated than that?

00:04:45.834 --> 00:04:51.223
But deciding on what traits define
a morphological species is so dependent on

00:04:51.223 --> 00:04:53.993
who is observing
and describing those traits.

00:04:54.443 --> 00:04:58.581
And it creates so many more questions
for how we use morphology

00:04:58.781 --> 00:05:01.433
to describe species and their taxonomy.

00:05:01.784 --> 00:05:04.453
What if the neck is a little shorter?

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:07.006
The body a little stouter?

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:09.408
Does that make a different species?

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:13.279
And just how much can we infer about
the relationships

00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:16.215
between two species
based on their appearance?

00:05:16.665 --> 00:05:20.269
If they look similar
does that mean they're close relatives,

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:24.523
or is that some kind of phylogenetic
optical illusion?

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:28.444
There's no single answer
to any of those questions.

00:05:28.961 --> 00:05:34.183
It all just depends on the organisms
and what it is you want to understand about them.

00:05:39.171 --> 00:05:41.924
Let's look at Lacrymaria sapropelica,

00:05:42.341 --> 00:05:44.993
a fellow member of 
the Lacrymaria genus.

00:05:45.577 --> 00:05:48.947
Its body is stout compared
to the Lacrymaria olor,

00:05:49.148 --> 00:05:52.601
like setting down a teapot
next to a champagne glass.

00:05:53.168 --> 00:05:57.706
Most importantly, its neck doesn't
seem to extend much beyond its body.

00:05:58.157 --> 00:06:02.694
Instead of the constant stretching 
movement of the Lacrymaria olor’s neck,

00:06:02.694 --> 00:06:07.366
the Lacrymaria sapropelica’s neck
neck is stubby and immobile.

00:06:07.883 --> 00:06:10.302
In fact, it looks a little bit like this

00:06:10.302 --> 00:06:12.921
other species called Phialina caudatum,

00:06:13.422 --> 00:06:18.827
which belongs to the genus Phialina, a genus
that closely resembles Lacrymaria

00:06:18.827 --> 00:06:21.864
except for the obvious issue of the neck.

00:06:22.247 --> 00:06:25.150
So if Lacrymaria sapropelica doesn't

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:28.720
have an extending neck,
why is it classified as

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.106
Lacrymaria and not Phialina?

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:36.795
Well, mostly because no one's taken 
the time to officially change it.

00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:41.867
Naming and classifying species
doesn't just happen automatically.

00:06:42.367 --> 00:06:44.603
A person has to come along and do it.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:47.322
Putting in the effort
to study the creatures

00:06:47.322 --> 00:06:50.025
and write up their reasons
for changing its identity.

00:06:50.659 --> 00:06:54.913
And, you know, sometimes
that person just hasn't come along. 

00:06:55.364 --> 00:06:57.082
That's the thing about people.

00:06:57.316 --> 00:07:01.036
We've come up with these
incredible systems for describing nature.

00:07:01.336 --> 00:07:07.392
But at the end of the day, those systems
are still subject to our very human whims.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.729
And just to emphasize how messy
the process

00:07:10.729 --> 00:07:15.617
of organizing organisms
is, we should add that Phialina caudatum

00:07:15.617 --> 00:07:20.138
actually went by another name
before Lacrymaria caudatum.

00:07:20.839 --> 00:07:23.208
But unlike Lacrymaria sapropelica,

00:07:23.542 --> 00:07:27.346
someone did come along
and do the work of revising its place.

00:07:27.796 --> 00:07:31.283
We don't know what motivated them
to take on the species.

00:07:31.783 --> 00:07:35.537
Maybe they were constantly irritated
when they read the words

00:07:35.537 --> 00:07:37.906
“Lacrymaria caudatum.”

00:07:37.906 --> 00:07:41.109
Maybe they just happened to be studying
the species already.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:44.029
Or they just wanted to publish a paper

00:07:44.713 --> 00:07:47.332
Or maybe it's a combination of all three.

00:07:47.899 --> 00:07:50.685
Human motivations and desires are just

00:07:50.685 --> 00:07:53.588
as difficult to categorize as microbes.

00:07:54.222 --> 00:07:58.927
All this messiness and subjectivity
seems built into morphology.

00:07:59.478 --> 00:08:03.315
But we live in the age of genetics
now where sequencing

00:08:03.315 --> 00:08:07.219
an organism is cheap
and the data objective, right?

00:08:08.119 --> 00:08:10.539
Yes and no.

00:08:10.539 --> 00:08:14.442
There are ways that molecular phylogeny
the use and comparison

00:08:14.442 --> 00:08:18.230
of genetic sequences
to understand evolutionary relationships

00:08:18.613 --> 00:08:23.051
has allowed us to see past the surface
similarities between organisms.

00:08:23.902 --> 00:08:26.888
Amoebas, for example,
were thought to be closely

00:08:26.888 --> 00:08:30.609
related because of their use of 
pseudopodia or false feet,

00:08:30.892 --> 00:08:35.180
but their genetic underpinnings revealed
that amoebas are actually

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:39.584
the culmination of many different species
and groups and phyla.

00:08:39.968 --> 00:08:44.139
They just happened to resemble each other
in this one major way.

00:08:44.356 --> 00:08:48.310
And those results, the specificity
they've allowed us in redefining these

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:51.379
species, have given us new understanding

00:08:51.379 --> 00:08:53.898
about their evolution and behaviors.

00:08:54.616 --> 00:08:57.669
But data, as concrete and undeniable as it

00:08:57.669 --> 00:09:00.939
feels, is still
in the eye of the beholder.

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:06.528
Like, let's say we found 
a new species with a short,

00:09:06.528 --> 00:09:10.665
not extending neck
and say we decided to name it.

00:09:10.699 --> 00:09:13.268
Lacrymaria microcosmosii.

00:09:13.902 --> 00:09:17.422
we're excited to find it,
and we're even more excited to study

00:09:17.422 --> 00:09:20.658
it and report all the genetic information
we can find

00:09:21.226 --> 00:09:24.462
describing the organism
in terms of its genetic code

00:09:24.829 --> 00:09:28.700
that we registered to a depository
for other scientists to look at.

00:09:29.167 --> 00:09:32.587
But then let's say that shortly
after our discovery

00:09:32.737 --> 00:09:35.390
other masters of microscopes
turn up their own

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:37.909
Lacrymaria microcosmosii discoveries.

00:09:38.476 --> 00:09:40.962
The morphologies
match what we've described.

00:09:41.513 --> 00:09:44.032
But when we compare our genetic sequences

00:09:44.032 --> 00:09:47.302
to what they've found,
there are differences.

00:09:48.136 --> 00:09:51.706
The question is how big do
those differences have to be

00:09:51.973 --> 00:09:54.259
to make the organism itself different?

00:09:54.893 --> 00:09:58.930
Do they define a new species,
an entirely new genus?

00:09:59.798 --> 00:10:02.050
At what point do we decide

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:07.088
something is too different
to be the same or even just similar?

00:10:08.056 --> 00:10:09.607
That's not necessarily a

00:10:09.607 --> 00:10:13.628
problem that DNA in computers
will be able to solve for us.

00:10:14.329 --> 00:10:19.784
There's still going to be a person who has
to decide, a person who has to make

00:10:19.784 --> 00:10:24.739
a subjective call based on the data
presented and the questions asked.

00:10:25.573 --> 00:10:28.943
And their definition
will probably have exceptions

00:10:28.943 --> 00:10:32.013
to leaving blanks, in our understanding.

00:10:32.597 --> 00:10:34.966
But blanks are exciting.

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:40.121
They leave us room for new ways
of seeing things, of asking things,

00:10:40.722 --> 00:10:45.259
because our definitions are an attempt
to bring the expansiveness of biology

00:10:45.493 --> 00:10:50.031
into something we can understand,
something we can speak and read.

00:10:50.682 --> 00:10:54.502
But life expands beyond
those constraints in ways

00:10:54.502 --> 00:10:57.472
we know, in ways we have yet to find out.

